   "blocks": [
    {
     "t": "h3",
     "text": "1.1 The Fourier-to-Laplace Convergence Bridge"
    },
    {
     "t": "p",
     "text": "The Continuous-Time Fourier Transform (CTFT) requires absolute integrability:"
    },
    {
     "t": "math",
     "tex": "\\int_{-\\infty}^\\infty \\vert x(t)\\vert dt < \\infty"
    },
    {
     "t": "p",
     "text": "Signals with exponential growth ($e^{2t}u(t)$) or polynomial growth ($t u(t)$) cannot be analyzed using CTFT because the Fourier integral diverges."
    },
    {
     "t": "p",
     "text": "To ensure convergence, $x(t)$ is multiplied by an exponential damping factor $e^{-\\sigma t}$:"
    },
    {
     "t": "math",
     "tex": "x_{\\text{damped}}(t) = x(t)e^{-\\sigma t}"
    },
    {
     "t": "p",
     "text": "Taking the CTFT of $x_{\\text{damped}}(t)$:"
    },
    {
     "t": "math",
     "tex": "\\mathcal{F}\\{x(t)e^{-\\sigma t}\\} = \\int_{-\\infty}^\\infty [x(t)e^{-\\sigma t}]e^{-j\\omega t}dt = \\int_{-\\infty}^\\infty x(t)e^{-(\\sigma + j\\omega)t}dt"
    },
    {
     "t": "p",
     "text": "Defining the **Complex Frequency Variable** $s$:"
    },
    {
     "t": "math",
     "tex": "s = \\sigma + j\\omega, \\quad \\sigma = \\text{Re}(s), \\quad \\omega = \\text{Im}(s)"
    },
    {
     "t": "p",
     "text": "This yields the **Bilateral (Two-Sided) Laplace Transform Analysis Equation**:"
    },
    {
     "t": "math",
     "tex": "X(s) = \\mathcal{L}\\{x(t)\\} = \\int_{-\\infty}^\\infty x(t)e^{-st}dt"
    },
    {
     "t": "h3",
     "text": "1.2 The Synthesis Equation (Bromwich Contour Integral)"
    },
    {
     "t": "p",
     "text": "Recovering the time signal from $X(s)$ is accomplished via the inverse Fourier transform of $X(\\sigma + j\\omega)$:"
    },
    {
     "t": "math",
     "tex": "x(t)e^{-\\sigma t} = \\frac{1}{2\\pi}\\int_{-\\infty}^\\infty X(\\sigma + j\\omega)e^{j\\omega t}d\\omega"
    },
    {
     "t": "p",
     "text": "Multiplying both sides by $e^{\\sigma t}$:"
    },
    {
     "t": "math",
     "tex": "x(t) = \\frac{1}{2\\pi}\\int_{-\\infty}^\\infty X(\\sigma + j\\omega)e^{(\\sigma + j\\omega)t}d\\omega"
    },
    {
     "t": "p",
     "text": "Since $s = \\sigma + j\\omega$, with $\\sigma$ held constant, $ds = j d\\omega \\implies d\\omega = \\frac{ds}{j}$. The limits $\\omega \\in [-\\infty, +\\infty]$ map to $s \\in [\\sigma - j\\infty, \\sigma + j\\infty]$:"
    },
    {
     "t": "math",
     "tex": "x(t) = \\frac{1}{2\\pi j}\\int_{\\sigma - j\\infty}^{\\sigma + j\\infty} X(s)e^{st}ds"
    },
    {
     "t": "p",
     "text": "where the vertical path of integration $\\text{Re}(s) = \\sigma$ must lie entirely within the Region of Convergence (ROC)."
    },
    {
     "t": "h3",
     "text": "1.3 Mapping Between Laplace and Fourier Transforms"
    },
    {
     "t": "math",
     "tex": "X(j\\omega) = \\left.X(s)\\right|_{s=j\\omega} = \\mathcal{F}\\{x(t)\\}"
    },
    {
     "t": "p",
     "text": "**Fundamental Condition:** This identity is valid **if and only if** the imaginary axis ($j\\omega$-axis, $\\sigma = 0$) lies strictly inside the Region of Convergence (ROC) of $X(s)$!"
    }
   ]
